Mastering Vim: Essential Commands for Linux Users

Vim is a powerful, widely used text editor in Linux, vital for developers and system administrators. Knowing Vim commands can drastically improve your efficiency when editing files in the terminal.

 Getting Started
vim filename.txt → Open a file
:q → Quit (if no changes)
:q! → Quit without saving
:wq → Save & quit
:w → Save without quitting

*************** Vim Modes ***********
1.Command Mode: Default mode for navigation & commands
2.Insert Mode: Press i to insert text
3.Visual Mode: Press v to select text

************ Navigation ***************
h/j/k/l → Move left/down/up/right
gg → Go to start of file
G → Go to end of file
:set number → Show line numbers

********** Editing Text*************
i → Insert before cursor
a → Insert after cursor
o → New line below
O → New line above
Esc → Back to command mode

********** Deleting Text*********
x → Delete character
dd → Delete line
dw → Delete to end of word
d$ → Delete to end of line

*********Copy & Paste*********
yy → Copy line
yw → Copy word
y$ → Copy to end of line
p → Paste after cursor
P → Paste before cursor

***************Undo & Redo********
u → Undo
Ctrl + r → Redo

********** Searching & Replacing **********
/search_term → Search
n → Next match
N → Previous match
:%s/old/new/g → Replace all
:%s/old/new/gc → Replace with confirmation

Vim might seem intimidating at first, but mastering these commands will make you a terminal productivity pro! 💪
